# Vue 3 Performance Best Practices

When writing or modifying frontend components (Vue/TS), strictly follow these performance strategies to avoid UI jank and unnecessary rendering overhead.

## 1. Handling Large Objects (`shallowRef`)

### Background
When receiving large, deeply nested JSON objects from the backend (e.g. full avatar details, game state snapshots), Vue's default reactivity system (`ref` or `reactive`) converts every property at every level into a Proxy. This happens synchronously and causes noticeable UI jank for complex objects (10ms - 100ms+).

### Solution
For these large, read-only data structures, you **must** use `shallowRef` instead of `ref`.

```typescript
import { shallowRef } from 'vue';

// ❌ BAD: deep conversion, very slow for large objects
const bigData = ref<ComplexType | null>(null);

// ✅ GOOD: only tracks changes to .value, no deep conversion
const bigData = shallowRef<ComplexType | null>(null);
```

### When to Use
- **Read-only display data**: data fetched from the API mainly for display, whose fields the frontend does not modify one by one.
- **Large lists/tree structures**: game state, logs, item lists, relationship graphs, etc.

### Important Trade-off
With `shallowRef`, **deep mutations do not trigger updates**.

```typescript
const data = shallowRef({ count: 1, nested: { name: 'foo' } });

// ❌ This will not update the UI
data.value.count++;
data.value.nested.name = 'bar';

// ✅ This updates the UI (replaces the whole object)
data.value = { ...data.value, count: data.value.count + 1 };
// Or assign the API response directly
data.value = apiResponse;
```

## 2. Component Rendering Optimization

### Virtual Scrolling
For long lists (e.g. event logs, entity lists), prefer "pagination + incremental loading" or virtual scrolling.
When the number of nodes rendered at once keeps growing or scrolling visibly stutters, virtual scrolling (rendering only the visible area) is mandatory to keep DOM overhead under control.

### Memoization
For expensive derived state, use `computed` instead of method calls or inline expressions in templates.

### Store Dependency Boundary
Components should depend on the domain store with the smallest responsibility (`map/avatar/event/ui/system`) and avoid unnecessarily reaching through to the aggregate store.

### Performance Baseline
Keep observable timing metrics for critical paths and compare them before and after refactors:
- Initialization time (e.g. the boot/init phase)
- Event merging and event loading time
